{"name":"Baked Salmon","hex":"#df9876","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baked","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of bake"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That has been cooked by baking.","Inebriated: drunk, high, or stoned."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baked"},{"term":"salmon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several species of fish, typically of the Salmoninae subfamily, brownish above with silvery sides and delicate pinkish-orange flesh; they ascend rivers to spawn.","A meal or dish made from this fish."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale pinkish-orange colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salmon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salmon as food","blocks":[{"text":"Salmon is a common food fish classified as an oily fish with a rich content of protein and omega-3 fatty acids. Norway is a major producer of farmed and wild salmon, accounting for more than 50% of global salmon production. Farmed and wild salmon differ only slightly in terms of food quality and safety, with farmed salmon having lower content of environmental contaminants, and wild salmon having higher content of omega-3 fatty acids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Sashimi_-_Maguro_Restaurant%2C_Bangna%2C_Bangkok_%2844856596864%29.jpg/330px-Sashimi_-_Maguro_Restaurant%2C_Bangna%2C_Bangkok_%2844856596864%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon_as_food"},{"title":"Baking","blocks":[{"text":"Baking is a method of preparing food that uses dry heat, typically in an oven, hot ashes, or on hot stones. The most commonly baked foods in 2025 were bread and rolls. Heat is gradually transferred from the surface of unbaked goods to their center, typically at elevated temperatures surpassing 300 °F. Dry heat cooking imparts a distinctive richness to foods through the processes of caramelization and surface browning. As heat travels through, it transforms batters and doughs into baked goods and more with a firm dry crust and a softer center. Baking can be combined with grilling to produce a hybrid barbecue variant by using both methods simultaneously, or one after the other. Baking is related to barbecuing because the concept of the masonry oven is similar to that of a smoke pit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Freshly_baked_bread_loaves.jpg/330px-Freshly_baked_bread_loaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baking"},{"title":"Salmon","blocks":[{"text":"Salmon are any of several commercially important species of ray-finned fish that can live in either freshwater or saltwater from the genera Salmo and Oncorhynchus of the family Salmonidae, native to tributaries of the North Atlantic (Salmo) and North Pacific (Oncorhynchus) basins. Salmon is a colloquial or common name used for fish in this group, but is not a scientific name. Other closely related fish in the same family include trout, char, grayling, whitefish, lenok and taimen, all coldwater fish of the subarctic and cooler temperate regions with some sporadic endorheic populations in Central Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Salmo_salar.jpg/330px-Salmo_salar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon"},{"title":"Salmon (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Salmon is a warm color ranging from light orange to pink, named after the color of salmon flesh."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/SockeyeMaleCloseup_%2814522094556%29.jpg/330px-SockeyeMaleCloseup_%2814522094556%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg/500px-%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg/1280px-%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg/500px-%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Still Life with Salmon, 1866–1869, by Édouard Manet, shows a white-fleshed salmon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%C3%89douard_Manet_-_Stilleben_mit_Lachs.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Salmon_Fish.JPG/500px-Salmon_Fish.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Salmon_Fish.JPG/1280px-Salmon_Fish.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Salmon_Fish.JPG/500px-Salmon_Fish.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salmon steak (left) and fillets (right) in a market","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalmon_Fish.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Gravad_lax.jpg/500px-Gravad_lax.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Gravad_lax.jpg/500px-Gravad_lax.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGravad_lax.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Lohikeit